sentences.text:
It would be apposite to refer to the decision of the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Sharad Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra 1984 4 SCC 116, wherein the "Panchsheel" or five principles of circumstantial evidence were laid down as follows:
(1) the circumstances from which the conclusion of guilt is to be drawn should be fully established;
(2) the facts so established should be consistent only with the hypothesis of the guilt of the accused, that is to say, they should not be explainable on any other hypothesis except that the accused is guilty;
(3) the circumstances should be of a conclusive nature and tendency;
(4) they should exclude every possible hypothesis except the one to be proved; and.
